WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.016
&gt;&gt; Thanks for coming.

00:00:06.016 --> 00:00:09.645
I'm very happy to introduce
Frank Wang from MIT.

00:00:09.646 --> 00:00:11.900
He's at PhD student of
the MIT CSAIL advised

00:00:11.901 --> 00:00:14.580
by Nickolai Zeldovich
and James Mickens.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.265
Frank works at
the intersection of

00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:17.950
systems and applied
cryptography.

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.450
And today he's going
to be telling us about

00:00:19.451 --> 00:00:20.980
his dissertation work
on protecting

00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:23.810
privacy in Web services.

00:00:23.811 --> 00:00:28.705
&gt;&gt; Thanks. Hi everyone,

00:00:28.706 --> 00:00:32.175
I'm Frank and I'm
a PhD student MIT CSAIL,

00:00:32.176 --> 00:00:35.480
and today I'm going to talk
about some my PhD research on

00:00:35.481 --> 00:00:37.290
building systems that protect

00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:40.170
sensitive user data
in Web services.

00:00:40.171 --> 00:00:42.900
So, whenever users interact

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:44.835
with Web services like Google,

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:47.405
Facebook, or Yelp they
provide their data.

00:00:47.406 --> 00:00:49.450
This data is
important and valuable

00:00:49.451 --> 00:00:51.960
both for the Web service
and the user.

00:00:51.961 --> 00:00:53.900
The user is willing to provide

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:56.170
her data because she
obtains value from it.

00:00:56.171 --> 00:00:59.840
For example, when a user
provides her Fitbit data,

00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:02.270
she can track
her health and exercise,

00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:04.665
as well as compare it
against other users.

00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:06.820
I mean, similar a user
provides her data

00:01:06.821 --> 00:01:08.860
to Facebook so that she can more

00:01:08.861 --> 00:01:10.820
easily learn about
the social lives of

00:01:10.821 --> 00:01:14.135
her friends and similarly
share her social life.

00:01:14.136 --> 00:01:17.640
Also buying things off
Amazon and thus giving

00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:19.140
your preferences can lead

00:01:19.141 --> 00:01:21.595
to better product
recommendations.

00:01:21.596 --> 00:01:23.340
But that's on the user side.

00:01:23.341 --> 00:01:25.270
So, on the other side,

00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:27.790
Web services actually gain
a lot of value from user data.

00:01:27.791 --> 00:01:30.400
As we know, Facebook has data on

00:01:30.401 --> 00:01:32.500
your preferences so you can

00:01:32.501 --> 00:01:35.205
make more money off ads
by targeting them better.

00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:36.965
Amazon can better provide

00:01:36.966 --> 00:01:40.860
product recommendations that
can lead to more purchases.

00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:45.090
So, however there's a big
security and privacy problem.

00:01:45.091 --> 00:01:49.030
Web services often handle
sensitive user data insecurely.

00:01:49.031 --> 00:01:53.580
So, at a very high level this
is how a Web service works.

00:01:53.581 --> 00:01:57.160
A user submits a request
on her mobile device,

00:01:57.161 --> 00:02:00.530
browser, or some client
to a service.

00:02:00.531 --> 00:02:04.580
The service processes
that request which might involve

00:02:04.581 --> 00:02:06.500
storing some user data
or performing

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:09.415
some computation and
returns a response.

00:02:09.416 --> 00:02:10.830
So, now service one has

00:02:10.831 --> 00:02:13.375
some user data or has
done some computation.

00:02:13.376 --> 00:02:17.900
And service one can then also
forward some of the data to

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:20.560
a second service
or second service

00:02:20.561 --> 00:02:23.585
might want to access data
from the first service.

00:02:23.586 --> 00:02:26.290
So, as you can see there are

00:02:26.291 --> 00:02:30.475
many channels where user data
can leak or be compromised.

00:02:30.476 --> 00:02:33.810
For example, data can leak on

00:02:33.811 --> 00:02:35.675
the user side
through the browser

00:02:35.676 --> 00:02:37.750
to an unauthorized application.

00:02:37.751 --> 00:02:41.005
Not properly handling
a query can also leak

00:02:41.006 --> 00:02:44.680
private information about
a user to a service.

00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:48.835
Unauthorized services can also
attempt to access the data

00:02:48.836 --> 00:02:50.600
and the data can leak from

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:53.585
the services' database and
kind of the list goes on.

00:02:53.586 --> 00:02:58.250
And this happens many times
because Web services

00:02:58.251 --> 00:03:01.390
often lacked cryptographically
secure mechanisms

00:03:01.391 --> 00:03:04.245
to protect private user data.

00:03:04.246 --> 00:03:06.750
So, kind of my research is very

00:03:06.751 --> 00:03:09.115
focused on solving
these types of problems.

00:03:09.116 --> 00:03:13.150
More broadly, it is
on building practical,

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:15.370
secure mechanisms to protect

00:03:15.371 --> 00:03:18.475
sensitive user data in
large scale Web services.

00:03:18.476 --> 00:03:21.660
So, naturally much
of my work lies

00:03:21.661 --> 00:03:25.605
at the intersection of
systems and cryptography.

00:03:25.606 --> 00:03:28.505
Because, as I'll show
in the next slide,

00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:30.820
in most of my research I apply

00:03:30.821 --> 00:03:32.570
crypto tools to build

00:03:32.571 --> 00:03:35.025
systems that help
prevent these data leaks.

00:03:35.026 --> 00:03:39.175
So, this is difficult for
two main reasons in my opinion.

00:03:39.176 --> 00:03:41.890
First, most of these
advanced crypto techniques

00:03:41.891 --> 00:03:44.765
are relatively new
and not well studied.

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:47.520
And second, It's
difficult to apply

00:03:47.521 --> 00:03:49.030
these crypto techniques in

00:03:49.031 --> 00:03:52.295
a practical way to achieve
the system's goals.

00:03:52.296 --> 00:03:54.580
So, as a result,

00:03:54.581 --> 00:03:57.080
I spent a lot of time with
cryptographers and, you know,

00:03:57.081 --> 00:03:58.330
I'm excited to continue

00:03:58.331 --> 00:04:02.305
these collaborations across
these two communities.

00:04:02.306 --> 00:04:06.800
So, recall the high
level representation

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:09.285
of a user-web
service interaction.

00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:12.300
So, this is how
my research projects aim

00:04:12.301 --> 00:04:15.530
to prevent data leaks
in some of the channels.

00:04:15.531 --> 00:04:17.980
So, many of the projects apply

00:04:17.981 --> 00:04:20.590
crypto techniques in
a practical manner

00:04:20.591 --> 00:04:22.155
to prevent these leaks.

00:04:22.156 --> 00:04:26.150
So, Veil is the
first platform that

00:04:26.151 --> 00:04:27.650
allows users to provide

00:04:27.651 --> 00:04:30.900
stronger private
browsing semantics.

00:04:30.901 --> 00:04:33.940
It focuses on
client side data leakage

00:04:33.941 --> 00:04:35.540
and it's based on the premise

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:37.270
that private browsing modes,

00:04:37.271 --> 00:04:39.690
like incognito mode,
are leaky and

00:04:39.691 --> 00:04:44.250
extremely difficult to
fix in the browser alone.

00:04:44.251 --> 00:04:47.060
My next project is Splinter,

00:04:47.061 --> 00:04:50.575
which allows for private
user queries to Web services,

00:04:50.576 --> 00:04:54.285
like Yelp or Google Maps
in a practical way.

00:04:54.286 --> 00:04:56.570
And my next few
projects are more

00:04:56.571 --> 00:04:59.010
focused on server side leakage.

00:04:59.011 --> 00:05:03.290
So, Cobweb and Riverbed
solve the problem that

00:05:03.291 --> 00:05:05.590
the privacy policies
for data and

00:05:05.591 --> 00:05:07.830
its derivatives are hard

00:05:07.831 --> 00:05:10.115
to enforce to
compact applications.

00:05:10.116 --> 00:05:11.930
So, we developed a practical

00:05:11.931 --> 00:05:13.885
information flow control system

00:05:13.886 --> 00:05:17.310
to deal with this in
modern Web services.

00:05:17.311 --> 00:05:20.010
And finally, Sieve provides

00:05:20.011 --> 00:05:23.680
cryptographically enforced
access control for user data.

00:05:23.681 --> 00:05:25.740
It's motivated by the fact that

00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:29.035
data is constantly shared
between applications.

00:05:29.036 --> 00:05:33.470
So, for today's talk I
will focus on Splinter,

00:05:33.471 --> 00:05:35.810
which had a strong
collaboration between

00:05:35.811 --> 00:05:39.000
cryptographers and
systems researchers.

00:05:39.001 --> 00:05:41.980
I worked with Shafi
and Vinod at MIT for

00:05:41.981 --> 00:05:45.490
the cryptography and Matei
for the systems aspect.

00:05:45.491 --> 00:05:49.595
And the reason I'm focusing
on this project is, first,

00:05:49.596 --> 00:05:52.160
we thought it was
really exciting to take

00:05:52.161 --> 00:05:54.800
a relatively recent
cryptographic tool and

00:05:54.801 --> 00:05:57.800
build an efficient end-to-end
private query system.

00:05:57.801 --> 00:06:01.745
And second, I enjoyed getting
two different groups of

00:06:01.746 --> 00:06:03.650
researchers and
together to work on

00:06:03.651 --> 00:06:06.150
a project they typically
would not work on.

00:06:06.151 --> 00:06:08.195
So, the systems people
did crypto work and

00:06:08.196 --> 00:06:10.885
the crypto people did
some systems work.

00:06:10.886 --> 00:06:13.690
So, with this
collaboration we're able to

00:06:13.691 --> 00:06:15.730
execute on this project

00:06:15.731 --> 00:06:18.705
relatively quickly
and efficiently.

00:06:18.706 --> 00:06:22.195
So, here's an outline
for the rest of my talk.

00:06:22.196 --> 00:06:23.750
I'll talk about Splinter,

00:06:23.751 --> 00:06:25.640
the motivation, threat model,

00:06:25.641 --> 00:06:27.510
how we execute Splinter queries,

00:06:27.511 --> 00:06:31.895
and finally the evaluation
and some future work.

00:06:31.896 --> 00:06:34.970
So, to kind of
motivate Splinter,

00:06:34.971 --> 00:06:36.770
suppose a user wants to query

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:40.635
a database to retrieve
records or perform analytics.

00:06:40.636 --> 00:06:43.120
Some examples of this
include searching for

00:06:43.121 --> 00:06:45.885
a patent to ensure your patent
hasn't been published.

00:06:45.886 --> 00:06:47.810
Looking for stock prices,

00:06:47.811 --> 00:06:52.170
medical databases to find
certain diseases or doctors,

00:06:52.171 --> 00:06:56.585
and Expedia to find
a flight for a trip.

00:06:56.586 --> 00:07:01.025
These queries leak
sensitive user information.

00:07:01.026 --> 00:07:04.580
For example, searching
for a patent on

00:07:04.581 --> 00:07:09.235
an outside database can reveal
ideas in your invention.

00:07:09.236 --> 00:07:11.420
An investors query
might indicate

00:07:11.421 --> 00:07:14.460
whether they plan to
buy or sell a stock.

00:07:14.461 --> 00:07:18.520
Even searching a medical
database to learn more about

00:07:18.521 --> 00:07:19.920
diseases and doctors could

00:07:19.921 --> 00:07:23.060
reveal and leak
potential health problems.

00:07:23.061 --> 00:07:25.260
And finally, Expedia could raise

00:07:25.261 --> 00:07:29.490
the prices of
frequently search flights.

00:07:29.491 --> 00:07:33.940
So, one naive way to
solve this problem,

00:07:33.941 --> 00:07:36.750
it's just for the user to
download the database from

00:07:36.751 --> 00:07:38.420
the service and perform

00:07:38.421 --> 00:07:40.515
the queries locally
on her machine.

00:07:40.516 --> 00:07:44.805
I mean, however these databases
like Google Maps, stocks,

00:07:44.806 --> 00:07:46.370
and patents are usually

00:07:46.371 --> 00:07:48.400
hundreds of megabytes
and the user has to

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:50.830
re-download any updates which

00:07:50.831 --> 00:07:53.165
leads to a high
communication costs.

00:07:53.166 --> 00:07:55.595
And that's especially
problematic

00:07:55.596 --> 00:07:57.580
for users on mobile phones.

00:07:57.581 --> 00:08:00.565
So, this brings up the question,

00:08:00.566 --> 00:08:02.940
how do we build
a practical system that can

00:08:02.941 --> 00:08:07.195
help keep user's
queries private?

00:08:07.196 --> 00:08:12.055
So, to accomplish this
with our system, Splinter,

00:08:12.056 --> 00:08:14.070
we considered a
slightly modified

00:08:14.071 --> 00:08:17.520
setting with more servers
and replicated databases.

00:08:17.521 --> 00:08:20.585
More formally, Splinter
has two principles.

00:08:20.586 --> 00:08:24.025
The user and multiple
autonomous providers and

00:08:24.026 --> 00:08:26.380
each provider has a copy

00:08:26.381 --> 00:08:29.770
of the same public database
in clear text.

00:08:29.771 --> 00:08:32.715
We assume, at least one provider

00:08:32.716 --> 00:08:34.555
does not collude
with the others.

00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:36.880
So, on the Web many
of these databases are

00:08:36.881 --> 00:08:39.290
public such as
OpenStreetMap, stocks,

00:08:39.291 --> 00:08:40.980
flights data, and, you know,

00:08:40.981 --> 00:08:42.350
for efficiency they are already

00:08:42.351 --> 00:08:45.670
replicated across
many providers.

00:08:45.671 --> 00:08:49.565
Also, unlike in
the naive approach,

00:08:49.566 --> 00:08:54.235
we pushed some computation
to the providers.

00:08:54.236 --> 00:08:57.630
So, what happens in Splinter?

00:08:57.631 --> 00:08:59.930
So, user splits her query into

00:08:59.931 --> 00:09:03.090
multiple parts and sends
them to the provider.

00:09:03.091 --> 00:09:06.960
And each provider processes
its share of the query

00:09:06.961 --> 00:09:11.365
on the database records and
then sends back a response.

00:09:11.366 --> 00:09:14.430
So, the user takes
all the responses

00:09:14.431 --> 00:09:17.490
from the providers
and combines them to

00:09:17.491 --> 00:09:20.110
get the result without
the providers learning

00:09:20.111 --> 00:09:25.010
their original query unless
all the providers collude.

00:09:25.011 --> 00:09:26.815
So, in Splinter,

00:09:26.816 --> 00:09:29.740
our queries have
low communication cost,

00:09:29.741 --> 00:09:32.485
computation, and
number of round trips.

00:09:32.486 --> 00:09:34.745
And this allows us achieve

00:09:34.746 --> 00:09:38.595
more practical performance
compared to previous systems.

00:09:38.596 --> 00:09:40.090
And to do this, we use

00:09:40.091 --> 00:09:42.360
a recent cryptographic
primitive function

00:09:42.361 --> 00:09:44.400
secret sharing to achieve this.

00:09:44.401 --> 00:09:46.410
And I'll talk more
about how this

00:09:46.411 --> 00:09:50.190
works later in the talk. Yes?

00:09:50.191 --> 00:09:52.200
&gt;&gt; So, here you
assume [inaudible]

00:09:52.201 --> 00:09:54.890
one provider who doesn't
collude to with others,

00:09:54.891 --> 00:09:55.980
so this will be secure, right?

00:09:55.981 --> 00:09:56.281
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:09:56.281 --> 00:09:58.330
&gt;&gt; So, if you relax
the assumption

00:09:58.331 --> 00:10:01.180
saying there's more than half
where they don't collude,

00:10:01.181 --> 00:10:02.900
will that give you

00:10:02.901 --> 00:10:04.370
a better performance or with

00:10:04.371 --> 00:10:06.785
a different crypto primitive?

00:10:06.786 --> 00:10:08.270
&gt;&gt; It's not clear what this

00:10:08.271 --> 00:10:09.985
crypto primitive
would look like.

00:10:09.986 --> 00:10:11.745
I mean, I think,
you can imagine,

00:10:11.746 --> 00:10:14.140
we explored using
kind of information

00:10:14.141 --> 00:10:16.775
theoretic private
information retrieval.

00:10:16.776 --> 00:10:18.150
That kind of has
different models.

00:10:18.151 --> 00:10:20.190
That has assumes
like a majority,

00:10:20.191 --> 00:10:21.610
or like a minority,

00:10:21.611 --> 00:10:25.020
doesn't collude else's models
where one doesn't collude.

00:10:25.021 --> 00:10:28.630
But I think function
secret sharing allows us to

00:10:28.631 --> 00:10:30.030
kind of get a lot there like

00:10:30.031 --> 00:10:32.270
good communication costs
and expressiveness.

00:10:32.271 --> 00:10:34.760
I mean, there could be kind
of something in the middle.

00:10:34.761 --> 00:10:36.230
I mean, I think that
crypto technique

00:10:36.231 --> 00:10:38.050
just doesn't exist yet.

00:10:38.051 --> 00:10:39.240
I mean, something worth looking

00:10:39.241 --> 00:10:42.790
into unlike
the theoretical side.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:48.705
Because now I've given you
an overview of Splinter.

00:10:48.706 --> 00:10:52.060
I want to more explicitly
state our threat model.

00:10:52.061 --> 00:10:54.565
So, the data on the providers,

00:10:54.566 --> 00:10:56.895
it's not sensitive
and in clear text.

00:10:56.896 --> 00:10:58.440
We assumed that providers are

00:10:58.441 --> 00:11:01.445
passive adversaries and try
to learn the users query,

00:11:01.446 --> 00:11:04.330
and they don't tamper with
the query or the data.

00:11:04.331 --> 00:11:06.490
And we also assume that

00:11:06.491 --> 00:11:10.470
at least one provider does
not collude with the others.

00:11:10.471 --> 00:11:15.525
So, the contributions of
this work are the following.

00:11:15.526 --> 00:11:19.065
Splinter, which is
a private query system

00:11:19.066 --> 00:11:20.690
for public databases that

00:11:20.691 --> 00:11:22.800
scales to realistic
applications.

00:11:22.801 --> 00:11:24.880
We are the first system that

00:11:24.881 --> 00:11:27.360
uses and extends
function secret sharing,

00:11:27.361 --> 00:11:31.975
FSS, with new protocols to
run complex private queries.

00:11:31.976 --> 00:11:34.660
We develop optimizations to

00:11:34.661 --> 00:11:36.725
lower CPU and
communication costs,

00:11:36.726 --> 00:11:41.230
and we evaluated our system
on realistic applications,

00:11:41.231 --> 00:11:43.755
which led to response times
of less than 1.6

00:11:43.756 --> 00:11:46.700
seconds for databases
with millions of records.

00:11:46.701 --> 00:11:48.840
And we have to have
up to an order of

00:11:48.841 --> 00:11:51.000
magnitude fewer round-trip times

00:11:51.001 --> 00:11:52.995
than prior systems
that are based on

00:11:52.996 --> 00:11:55.070
cryptographic techniques such as

00:11:55.071 --> 00:11:58.960
PIR and Garbled circuits.

00:11:58.961 --> 00:12:03.540
So now, I've given you
a motivation and threat model.

00:12:03.541 --> 00:12:05.230
I'll move on to how
we actually apply

00:12:05.231 --> 00:12:08.245
FSS to execute Splinter queries.

00:12:08.246 --> 00:12:11.730
So, here's the query format
that we support.

00:12:11.731 --> 00:12:15.430
We support a subset of SQL
specifically projections,

00:12:15.431 --> 00:12:18.455
limiting filters,
aggregates, and no joins.

00:12:18.456 --> 00:12:21.090
We support queries in
the following format.

00:12:21.091 --> 00:12:24.295
Select multiple
aggregates or projections

00:12:24.296 --> 00:12:26.760
from a single table

00:12:26.761 --> 00:12:29.470
where certain conditions
hold and group by or order

00:12:29.471 --> 00:12:32.750
by multiple expressions
with a specific limit.

00:12:32.751 --> 00:12:36.120
It's important to note
that Splinter's goals is

00:12:36.121 --> 00:12:38.390
to keep the
sensitive information

00:12:38.391 --> 00:12:39.745
in the condition private,

00:12:39.746 --> 00:12:43.645
and shown by in
the red box with the lock.

00:12:43.646 --> 00:12:46.470
And so, a more
concrete example is,

00:12:46.471 --> 00:12:48.605
if we were to search for

00:12:48.606 --> 00:12:50.550
the top 10 cheapest flights

00:12:50.551 --> 00:12:52.035
for a certain route on Expedia.

00:12:52.036 --> 00:12:54.230
This is kind of
an example query we'd run,

00:12:54.231 --> 00:12:56.300
and the condition route
equals five,

00:12:56.301 --> 00:12:58.820
which is just an encoding
of the route,

00:12:58.821 --> 00:13:02.575
leaks information about
the route we are searching for.

00:13:02.576 --> 00:13:06.290
And so, we want to
hide the number five.

00:13:06.291 --> 00:13:08.450
And so, in the next slide,

00:13:08.451 --> 00:13:10.635
I'll show you what conditions
Splinter supports.

00:13:10.636 --> 00:13:13.040
And later I'll show
you how we execute

00:13:13.041 --> 00:13:15.330
this exact query while

00:13:15.331 --> 00:13:18.350
hiding five and
preserving privacy.

00:13:18.351 --> 00:13:21.000
So, Splinter supports
three types of

00:13:21.001 --> 00:13:22.750
conditions in the query filter.

00:13:22.751 --> 00:13:26.255
The following table is
important because the algorithm

00:13:26.256 --> 00:13:27.700
that Splinter executes for

00:13:27.701 --> 00:13:30.795
query aggregates depends
on the condition type.

00:13:30.796 --> 00:13:33.640
And so, the three conditions
are equality-only,

00:13:33.641 --> 00:13:35.840
intervals, and disjoint ORs.

00:13:35.841 --> 00:13:38.390
The form of the condition
is in the right column.

00:13:38.391 --> 00:13:40.135
E represents the column name,

00:13:40.136 --> 00:13:45.240
and Ci can be inequality
or interval condition.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:50.230
So, Splinters is
the first system to use function

00:13:50.231 --> 00:13:51.780
secret sharing FSS as

00:13:51.781 --> 00:13:53.785
a building block
for private queries.

00:13:53.786 --> 00:13:56.120
But, before we describe how

00:13:56.121 --> 00:13:58.970
to actually execute
these queries,

00:13:58.971 --> 00:14:01.870
I'll describe some of
the important features of FSS.

00:14:01.871 --> 00:14:06.440
So, the goal of FSS is
to evaluate a function in

00:14:06.441 --> 00:14:08.370
a distributed fashion without

00:14:08.371 --> 00:14:11.065
the providers learning
the actual function.

00:14:11.066 --> 00:14:14.045
And so, how does
this happen in FSS?

00:14:14.046 --> 00:14:17.705
So first, a function is
divided into k shares,

00:14:17.706 --> 00:14:20.000
and these shares have
the following properties.

00:14:20.001 --> 00:14:22.475
Each share can be
evaluated quickly,

00:14:22.476 --> 00:14:24.770
the sum of the shares
evaluated on

00:14:24.771 --> 00:14:27.150
an input is equal to the input

00:14:27.151 --> 00:14:30.010
evaluated on
the original function

00:14:30.011 --> 00:14:31.830
and given all but one share,

00:14:31.831 --> 00:14:37.710
someone can't recover
the original function f. So,

00:14:37.711 --> 00:14:40.015
there are efficient
constructions for two cases.

00:14:40.016 --> 00:14:41.825
First, point functions,

00:14:41.826 --> 00:14:43.430
which is a function
that evaluates to

00:14:43.431 --> 00:14:47.120
one if x is a specific value
and zero otherwise.

00:14:47.121 --> 00:14:48.670
And for Interval functions,

00:14:48.671 --> 00:14:51.010
is a function that
evaluates to one if x is

00:14:51.011 --> 00:14:53.615
between two numbers
and zero otherwise.

00:14:53.616 --> 00:14:56.340
So, as you can see, kind
of at a very high level,

00:14:56.341 --> 00:14:57.720
we want to transform

00:14:57.721 --> 00:15:01.315
the query conditions into
FSS supported functions,

00:15:01.316 --> 00:15:03.820
and execute them on
the database with

00:15:03.821 --> 00:15:07.145
specific protocols on top
of that to preserve privacy.

00:15:07.146 --> 00:15:09.060
And in the next few slides,

00:15:09.061 --> 00:15:11.995
I will give you
some specific examples,

00:15:11.996 --> 00:15:15.535
how we go about
executing these queries.

00:15:15.536 --> 00:15:18.420
So, to better understand how FSS

00:15:18.421 --> 00:15:20.395
works in the context
of Splinter,

00:15:20.396 --> 00:15:23.175
I'll start with how we
perform a COUNT query.

00:15:23.176 --> 00:15:25.380
And so, suppose we

00:15:25.381 --> 00:15:28.270
want to know the number of
flights for a certain route.

00:15:28.271 --> 00:15:31.240
For simplicity, let's
consider a subset of

00:15:31.241 --> 00:15:34.600
a flight database with
two columns, route and Price.

00:15:34.601 --> 00:15:39.565
Each provider holds a copy
of this database like that.

00:15:39.566 --> 00:15:43.210
And so, suppose we want to
count the number of flights.

00:15:43.211 --> 00:15:46.010
Where the route is
five but we don't want

00:15:46.011 --> 00:15:49.965
the provider to learn that
we're querying for route five.

00:15:49.966 --> 00:15:54.780
And so, for reference when
we run this specific query,

00:15:54.781 --> 00:15:57.340
the answer to
this question is two.

00:15:57.341 --> 00:16:00.575
And so, how does Splinter
perform this query?

00:16:00.576 --> 00:16:03.110
Well, first, we
need a function that

00:16:03.111 --> 00:16:05.420
evaluates to one if
the input is five and

00:16:05.421 --> 00:16:08.855
zero otherwise and
then the user uses

00:16:08.856 --> 00:16:11.335
FSS to convert this function

00:16:11.336 --> 00:16:14.405
into function shares for
the number of providers.

00:16:14.406 --> 00:16:16.050
And in this example,

00:16:16.051 --> 00:16:18.090
there are two providers.

00:16:18.091 --> 00:16:21.285
Some important properties
of the function shares,

00:16:21.286 --> 00:16:27.000
f1 and f2 are that one of x
plus f2 of x equals f of x.

00:16:27.001 --> 00:16:31.580
And having either f1
or f2 doesn't reveal

00:16:31.581 --> 00:16:33.350
any information
about the original

00:16:33.351 --> 00:16:36.750
f which contains
a user's sensitive information,

00:16:36.751 --> 00:16:39.035
our value of five.

00:16:39.036 --> 00:16:41.080
So, each provider applies

00:16:41.081 --> 00:16:44.295
its share to each route record
in the database,

00:16:44.296 --> 00:16:46.350
and it's important to note

00:16:46.351 --> 00:16:48.030
that the provider
has to scan through

00:16:48.031 --> 00:16:49.580
every entry in the database or

00:16:49.581 --> 00:16:51.360
else we leak user information.

00:16:51.361 --> 00:16:53.140
So, here are some examples of

00:16:53.141 --> 00:16:55.830
values that can be output
by the function share.

00:16:55.831 --> 00:16:57.510
All these operations are done

00:16:57.511 --> 00:17:00.510
modulo and integer
field but in our case,

00:17:00.511 --> 00:17:03.440
we use negatives for
illustration purposes.

00:17:03.441 --> 00:17:06.955
And so, each provider
sums together

00:17:06.956 --> 00:17:09.830
the outputs of
the evaluated functions

00:17:09.831 --> 00:17:11.760
and sends them back to the user.

00:17:11.761 --> 00:17:17.060
In this case, one provider
sums up 21 and sends back 21,

00:17:17.061 --> 00:17:18.200
and the other one
sums up negative

00:17:18.201 --> 00:17:21.030
19 and sends that
back to the user,

00:17:21.031 --> 00:17:22.790
who then sums them together

00:17:22.791 --> 00:17:26.170
to obtain the answer
to our original query.

00:17:26.171 --> 00:17:28.320
And so, why does this work?

00:17:28.321 --> 00:17:33.630
Recall that f1 of x plus
f2 of x equals f of x.

00:17:33.631 --> 00:17:35.825
This means in our example,

00:17:35.826 --> 00:17:40.080
given our function f of x
equals one if x equals five.

00:17:40.081 --> 00:17:44.360
Across providers,
the values will sum to one,

00:17:44.361 --> 00:17:46.405
if the route is equal to five,

00:17:46.406 --> 00:17:52.025
and since f of x equal
to zero if x is not five,

00:17:52.026 --> 00:17:54.560
across providers
the values will sum to

00:17:54.561 --> 00:17:58.280
zero if the route
is not five. Yes?

00:17:58.281 --> 00:18:01.010
&gt;&gt; Can f1 be zero?

00:18:01.011 --> 00:18:01.800
&gt;&gt; I mean.

00:18:01.801 --> 00:18:03.995
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:18:03.996 --> 00:18:07.000
&gt;&gt; Yes, what it outputs
is actually random.

00:18:07.001 --> 00:18:08.670
So, it can be zero.

00:18:08.671 --> 00:18:10.520
It's modular integer field,

00:18:10.521 --> 00:18:16.835
so it's like zero to n -
1 in the integer field.

00:18:16.836 --> 00:18:18.995
An integer field has
n elements, right?

00:18:18.996 --> 00:18:21.760
And so it can be zero to n - 1.

00:18:21.761 --> 00:18:24.130
I mean, I use negative numbers
here just to kind

00:18:24.131 --> 00:18:27.730
of make things easier but
it's all mod something.

00:18:27.731 --> 00:18:36.890
&gt;&gt; Can you do it
with real numbers?

00:18:36.891 --> 00:18:40.095
&gt;&gt; So real numbers are
a little bit trickier.

00:18:40.096 --> 00:18:42.385
You have to convert them into

00:18:42.386 --> 00:18:46.545
an integer first or you have
to hash them in some way.

00:18:46.546 --> 00:18:49.050
FSS works best when

00:18:49.051 --> 00:18:51.320
the kind of input is
an actual integer.

00:18:51.321 --> 00:18:53.160
I mean, real numbers,

00:18:53.161 --> 00:18:55.390
you can imagine ways to map

00:18:55.391 --> 00:18:59.270
some real numbers to
integers potentially.

00:18:59.271 --> 00:19:04.150
Cool. Great. So now,

00:19:04.151 --> 00:19:06.765
suppose that we want
to perform a SUM query,

00:19:06.766 --> 00:19:08.610
say because we want to find

00:19:08.611 --> 00:19:11.100
the average price
for a specific route.

00:19:11.101 --> 00:19:13.470
So, how do we do this?

00:19:13.471 --> 00:19:16.180
It's an extension of COUNT,

00:19:16.181 --> 00:19:18.850
and so we want to use
the same setup except

00:19:18.851 --> 00:19:20.560
we want to do a

00:19:20.561 --> 00:19:23.415
SUM(price) instead of
COUNT in the SQL query.

00:19:23.416 --> 00:19:26.950
On the providers, we do
the same thing as COUNT,

00:19:26.951 --> 00:19:28.640
except in addition to

00:19:28.641 --> 00:19:31.200
evaluating the function share
on each route record

00:19:31.201 --> 00:19:33.230
we also multiplied the output of

00:19:33.231 --> 00:19:35.990
the function share with
the corresponding price.

00:19:35.991 --> 00:19:38.885
And because FSS is
an additive function,

00:19:38.886 --> 00:19:41.710
this essentially
has the effect of

00:19:41.711 --> 00:19:45.425
scaling the matching
records by the price.

00:19:45.426 --> 00:19:49.535
And then each provider
adds up all the results,

00:19:49.536 --> 00:19:51.150
sends them back to the user,

00:19:51.151 --> 00:19:52.470
who sums them up, and gets

00:19:52.471 --> 00:19:55.500
the final answer to their query.

00:19:55.501 --> 00:19:59.180
So now, we've seen
how to do two types

00:19:59.181 --> 00:20:01.610
of additive queries,
COUNT and SUM.

00:20:01.611 --> 00:20:04.420
Let's look at how we do
a non-additive query,

00:20:04.421 --> 00:20:07.060
MIN, with an
Equality-Only condition.

00:20:07.061 --> 00:20:12.320
So, consider the same table
as the count in SUM query,

00:20:12.321 --> 00:20:14.570
and now we want to
find the price of

00:20:14.571 --> 00:20:18.570
the cheapest flight with
route five and height five.

00:20:18.571 --> 00:20:21.340
Now, we use the same
function as before,

00:20:21.341 --> 00:20:24.655
generate shares, and send
them to the provider.

00:20:24.656 --> 00:20:28.670
So using FSS is not enough
to execute this query in

00:20:28.671 --> 00:20:31.170
this particular case because

00:20:31.171 --> 00:20:32.970
it emphasizes
an additive function.

00:20:32.971 --> 00:20:35.080
So it's not clear
how it's directly

00:20:35.081 --> 00:20:38.430
compatible with
non-additive queries like MIN.

00:20:38.431 --> 00:20:41.420
And so, how do we
perform this query?

00:20:41.421 --> 00:20:45.280
So our observation is
that the provider can use

00:20:45.281 --> 00:20:47.100
the public information in

00:20:47.101 --> 00:20:50.790
the user-provided query
to form another query.

00:20:50.791 --> 00:20:53.320
More specifically,
in our example,

00:20:53.321 --> 00:20:56.100
the provider performs
"SELECT MIN(price),

00:20:56.101 --> 00:20:58.360
GROUP BY route" and

00:20:58.361 --> 00:21:01.180
this query creates
an intermediate table,

00:21:01.181 --> 00:21:05.390
which has the MIN price
for each distinct route.

00:21:05.391 --> 00:21:07.720
And because this table
was created using

00:21:07.721 --> 00:21:10.230
a query based on
public information,

00:21:10.231 --> 00:21:12.660
no sensitive user
information is leaked.

00:21:12.661 --> 00:21:15.925
And computing this table
takes linear time.

00:21:15.926 --> 00:21:18.640
Then we do the
same thing as SUM.

00:21:18.641 --> 00:21:21.055
Apply the function shares to

00:21:21.056 --> 00:21:22.705
all the records in
the route column

00:21:22.706 --> 00:21:24.210
in the intermediate table,

00:21:24.211 --> 00:21:26.040
and multiplied the MIN(price)

00:21:26.041 --> 00:21:29.225
column with the output
of each function share.

00:21:29.226 --> 00:21:31.100
Each provider then adds up

00:21:31.101 --> 00:21:36.280
all these values and sends
the response back to the user.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:41.640
So, now let's try
to do a MIN query

00:21:41.641 --> 00:21:44.160
with the different
condition type intervals.

00:21:44.161 --> 00:21:46.280
So again, using FSS is

00:21:46.281 --> 00:21:48.595
not clear here because
of the MIN aggregate.

00:21:48.596 --> 00:21:50.930
But now, also
the condition now contains

00:21:50.931 --> 00:21:52.770
multiple values that we

00:21:52.771 --> 00:21:54.770
want to match rather
than a single value,

00:21:54.771 --> 00:21:56.860
like the quality-only condition.

00:21:56.861 --> 00:22:01.210
And so one naive way to
perform this is to just

00:22:01.211 --> 00:22:02.750
perform the previous protocol we

00:22:02.751 --> 00:22:05.375
described, a multiple values.

00:22:05.376 --> 00:22:08.090
But that doesn't scale
well to larger interval

00:22:08.091 --> 00:22:09.600
ranges because we now have to

00:22:09.601 --> 00:22:11.325
do many of these round trips.

00:22:11.326 --> 00:22:13.270
So how do we kind
of do this with

00:22:13.271 --> 00:22:16.035
a limited number of round trips?

00:22:16.036 --> 00:22:18.860
And so let's say
we want to select

00:22:18.861 --> 00:22:21.860
the minimum price of
several similar routes.

00:22:21.861 --> 00:22:23.160
And so now I'll provide

00:22:23.161 --> 00:22:25.030
a high-level overview
of this protocol.

00:22:25.031 --> 00:22:30.820
So first, each provider uses
the public information in

00:22:30.821 --> 00:22:33.365
the query and they compute

00:22:33.366 --> 00:22:37.340
a sorted table T using
the SQL query "SELECT route,

00:22:37.341 --> 00:22:40.455
price ORDER BY route".

00:22:40.456 --> 00:22:43.620
Then, the providers find

00:22:43.621 --> 00:22:46.310
the MIN price on
power-of-2 intervals in

00:22:46.311 --> 00:22:48.440
the sorted table T. So here

00:22:48.441 --> 00:22:51.010
for illustration purposes
and simplicity,

00:22:51.011 --> 00:22:52.650
we just showed
the price column in

00:22:52.651 --> 00:22:55.400
our table T. So
those numbers just

00:22:55.401 --> 00:22:57.290
represent the price column that

00:22:57.291 --> 00:23:00.980
correspond to the sorted routes.

00:23:00.981 --> 00:23:04.690
So for our sorted table
of eight values,

00:23:04.691 --> 00:23:08.105
we create size two up
to size eight intervals.

00:23:08.106 --> 00:23:10.235
And so for the size
two intervals,

00:23:10.236 --> 00:23:12.600
you can see we just look
at neighboring pairs

00:23:12.601 --> 00:23:15.045
of values in T and
pick the minimum,

00:23:15.046 --> 00:23:17.310
and then we continue
this process

00:23:17.311 --> 00:23:20.535
up to size eight intervals.

00:23:20.536 --> 00:23:22.840
And so we have a small table
but you can kind of

00:23:22.841 --> 00:23:25.240
see how we can do this

00:23:25.241 --> 00:23:30.050
iteratively for
larger tables. Yes.

00:23:30.051 --> 00:23:33.230
&gt;&gt; Your route number
is being consecutive?

00:23:33.231 --> 00:23:36.435
&gt;&gt; So our route numbers,

00:23:36.436 --> 00:23:39.350
so we sort this ahead
of time. So, yeah.

00:23:39.351 --> 00:23:41.460
&gt;&gt; So it means you can't have

00:23:41.461 --> 00:23:44.015
huge gaps in your rep numbers
or something like that.

00:23:44.016 --> 00:23:46.065
Like one of them have a 101,

00:23:46.066 --> 00:23:48.445
three, 24, something like that.

00:23:48.446 --> 00:23:50.260
You would have to fill in?

00:23:50.261 --> 00:23:51.845
&gt;&gt; Oh, no, no. We don't. So this

00:23:51.846 --> 00:23:53.770
is just the price column.

00:23:53.771 --> 00:23:55.725
So the route just kind
of corresponds to this.

00:23:55.726 --> 00:23:59.170
Your route can kind
of be any range.

00:23:59.171 --> 00:24:04.305
&gt;&gt; A user needs to know
how to sort of map?

00:24:04.306 --> 00:24:09.060
Okay, so then we'll
be as deep though as,

00:24:09.061 --> 00:24:13.330
it'll be sort of as if,
if the user doesn't know,

00:24:13.331 --> 00:24:15.450
I guess, what
the route's numbers

00:24:15.451 --> 00:24:17.225
are, then do you have a problem?

00:24:17.226 --> 00:24:18.580
&gt;&gt; No, so the user doesn't

00:24:18.581 --> 00:24:21.320
actually have to know
anything about this table.

00:24:21.321 --> 00:24:23.935
Now, I kind of show,
the next slide tells you

00:24:23.936 --> 00:24:26.920
what the server does to
kind of get the right value.

00:24:26.921 --> 00:24:29.995
So here the routes can
be kind of anything,

00:24:29.996 --> 00:24:32.180
like the routes
can have big gaps

00:24:32.181 --> 00:24:34.125
or not big gaps or can
be close to each other.

00:24:34.126 --> 00:24:35.480
I mean, it doesn't matter

00:24:35.481 --> 00:24:37.255
because we just sort
this ahead of time.

00:24:37.256 --> 00:24:38.790
The server knows
we're doing a MIN with

00:24:38.791 --> 00:24:40.270
an interval query and kind

00:24:40.271 --> 00:24:43.470
of performs a sorted
where it caches it and so,

00:24:43.471 --> 00:24:46.580
this is kind of what the server
does in the first step,

00:24:46.581 --> 00:24:50.285
is kind of create these up
to size eight intervals.

00:24:50.286 --> 00:24:52.890
In the next slide I'll show
you kind of what the server

00:24:52.891 --> 00:24:57.800
does to return
the values back to.

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:03.295
&gt;&gt; So for size two intervals,

00:25:03.296 --> 00:25:05.010
like that second line,

00:25:05.011 --> 00:25:07.125
the first bucket will

00:25:07.126 --> 00:25:08.540
be whatever the first
two route numbers are,

00:25:08.541 --> 00:25:10.110
no matter if it be one

00:25:10.111 --> 00:25:12.040
and the 101 if that's
what they were, right?

00:25:12.041 --> 00:25:13.815
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. I'll show
in the next one how we kind

00:25:13.816 --> 00:25:15.710
of resolve a lot of this.

00:25:15.711 --> 00:25:17.090
&gt;&gt; To get into that,

00:25:17.091 --> 00:25:18.235
we have this price, 0, that's

00:25:18.236 --> 00:25:19.640
because that route is missing?

00:25:19.641 --> 00:25:22.380
&gt;&gt; I mean, this is an example
I made. Or it's free.

00:25:22.381 --> 00:25:23.475
&gt;&gt; But you won't have a

00:25:23.476 --> 00:25:25.440
zero filled in for
a route that is missing?

00:25:25.441 --> 00:25:27.125
&gt;&gt; Sorry?

00:25:27.126 --> 00:25:28.855
&gt;&gt; So all these are eight,

00:25:28.856 --> 00:25:30.330
they all have a route.

00:25:30.331 --> 00:25:30.801
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:30.801 --> 00:25:32.710
&gt;&gt; What if you have
only seven routes?

00:25:32.711 --> 00:25:35.280
What do you do with
the eighth item?

00:25:35.281 --> 00:25:37.380
&gt;&gt; I mean yeah, so
if I only have eight,

00:25:37.381 --> 00:25:39.550
I mean if there was
kind of like a gap here,

00:25:39.551 --> 00:25:41.460
you kind of still
create up to size

00:25:41.461 --> 00:25:44.215
eight intervals. You
can still do this.

00:25:44.216 --> 00:25:46.270
So here, let's say we're

00:25:46.271 --> 00:25:49.285
missing zero, then
this will just be one.

00:25:49.286 --> 00:25:52.115
Right I mean we kind
of just size like that.

00:25:52.116 --> 00:25:53.870
And so, I mean, It's

00:25:53.871 --> 00:25:55.470
definitely easier
if its power-of-two

00:25:55.471 --> 00:25:59.020
but you kind of imagine
if it goes out here,

00:25:59.021 --> 00:26:00.390
like we want to
do it to the next.

00:26:00.391 --> 00:26:03.780
So if there are nine,
there will be up to, like,

00:26:03.781 --> 00:26:11.025
size 16. Okay, cool.

00:26:11.026 --> 00:26:14.380
So, yeah. Then the algorithm
proceeds in two rounds.

00:26:14.381 --> 00:26:15.590
So in the first round,

00:26:15.591 --> 00:26:18.600
the user wants to find

00:26:18.601 --> 00:26:20.690
the maximum and
minimum indices in

00:26:20.691 --> 00:26:23.930
the price column where the route
is between one and five,

00:26:23.931 --> 00:26:25.320
in this particular example.

00:26:25.321 --> 00:26:27.460
And this requires two parallel

00:26:27.461 --> 00:26:30.090
FSS point function queries.

00:26:30.091 --> 00:26:31.300
And so, for this example,

00:26:31.301 --> 00:26:34.320
we find out that
the MIN and MAX database indices

00:26:34.321 --> 00:26:36.230
for those specific routes are,

00:26:36.231 --> 00:26:37.680
say, three and six.

00:26:37.681 --> 00:26:39.935
And so, in the next round,

00:26:39.936 --> 00:26:43.215
the client selects at
most two intervals of

00:26:43.216 --> 00:26:45.260
each size to cover

00:26:45.261 --> 00:26:48.475
the target interval
and returns a result.

00:26:48.476 --> 00:26:50.170
And in this case we

00:26:50.171 --> 00:26:53.730
select two intervals
from the sorted table T,

00:26:53.731 --> 00:26:58.235
marked in yellow, and one
from the size two interval,

00:26:58.236 --> 00:27:01.700
marked in yellow, to

00:27:01.701 --> 00:27:04.300
cover the targeted
interval three to six,

00:27:04.301 --> 00:27:06.050
and then we choose
two and zero from

00:27:06.051 --> 00:27:09.430
the sorted table T. So it's
important to note that,

00:27:09.431 --> 00:27:11.100
you know, we can't just query

00:27:11.101 --> 00:27:14.220
these three yellow
values directly.

00:27:14.221 --> 00:27:15.840
If we do, the adversary will

00:27:15.841 --> 00:27:18.550
learn our interval conditions.

00:27:18.551 --> 00:27:20.560
So that's why we have to issue

00:27:20.561 --> 00:27:23.355
FSS queries for
each interval table,

00:27:23.356 --> 00:27:26.025
so that the server doesn't
know what we're searching for.

00:27:26.026 --> 00:27:27.260
But the nice thing is

00:27:27.261 --> 00:27:30.500
that we can just do
this in parallel.

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:37.540
And so, in the last
few slides I showed you

00:27:37.541 --> 00:27:38.810
how Splinter can execute

00:27:38.811 --> 00:27:40.930
additive aggregates
like COUNT and SUM,

00:27:40.931 --> 00:27:43.020
and non-additive
aggregates like MAX, MIN,

00:27:43.021 --> 00:27:45.975
and TOPK for equality-only
interval conditions.

00:27:45.976 --> 00:27:48.380
I won't have time to
describe how would you do

00:27:48.381 --> 00:27:50.990
non-aggregates with
disjoint OR conditions.

00:27:50.991 --> 00:27:52.685
But at a very high level,

00:27:52.686 --> 00:27:55.080
we use private binary search and

00:27:55.081 --> 00:27:59.920
sampling on top of FSS
to handle these queries.

