00:50:44.966 --> 00:50:49.410
Yeah. I think they
use when one of these

00:50:49.411 --> 00:50:56.325
like IT PIR kind of like
libraries out there so.

00:50:59.085 --> 00:51:02.925
Cool. Yeah, so there's
been a bunch of

00:51:02.926 --> 00:51:05.245
work on practical
private query systems.

00:51:05.246 --> 00:51:07.755
And Splinter is
also related to work

00:51:07.756 --> 00:51:10.615
on PIR systems and
garbled circuits.

00:51:10.616 --> 00:51:12.285
But Splinter is more

00:51:12.286 --> 00:51:15.035
practical compared to
those other systems.

00:51:15.036 --> 00:51:18.485
The practical
private query systems

00:51:18.486 --> 00:51:21.435
suffer from some combination
of limited functionality,

00:51:21.436 --> 00:51:24.660
heavy leakage, and bad scaling.

00:51:24.661 --> 00:51:29.565
In PIR, a user can privately
query for a relaxed record,

00:51:29.566 --> 00:51:32.005
or for a record at index
I without the servers

00:51:32.006 --> 00:51:34.635
learning the index
or the response.

00:51:34.636 --> 00:51:37.495
However, not much work
has been done on building

00:51:37.496 --> 00:51:39.645
very practical PIR
systems that can

00:51:39.646 --> 00:51:42.125
perform realistic
application queries.

00:51:42.126 --> 00:51:44.935
Typically state of
the art PIR systems use

00:51:44.936 --> 00:51:46.715
multiparty information through

00:51:46.716 --> 00:51:48.565
PIR for performance reasons.

00:51:48.566 --> 00:51:50.585
And even though with
that, these systems

00:51:50.586 --> 00:51:52.560
require many round trips,

00:51:52.561 --> 00:51:54.165
high communication costs for

00:51:54.166 --> 00:51:56.650
the type of queries
that Splinter supports.

00:51:56.651 --> 00:51:59.225
But because we use FSS,

00:51:59.226 --> 00:52:01.465
we'd get around a lot of
these performance issues.

00:52:01.466 --> 00:52:05.820
And with information
theoretic PIR,

00:52:05.821 --> 00:52:07.435
to get the performance
that require

00:52:07.436 --> 00:52:09.250
that no priors collude,

00:52:09.251 --> 00:52:11.165
while in Splinter, we allow

00:52:11.166 --> 00:52:13.620
all but one to collude
with each other.

00:52:13.621 --> 00:52:17.635
Isn't PIR just one provider
or multiple providers.

00:52:17.636 --> 00:52:20.175
&gt;&gt; There's different
PIR kind of schemes.

00:52:20.176 --> 00:52:22.190
So, the more efficient
ones obviously,

00:52:22.191 --> 00:52:24.595
they assume none of the
providers can collude but there's

00:52:24.596 --> 00:52:27.025
a lot of, information theoretic,

00:52:27.026 --> 00:52:30.305
it's the very common ones
either like a minority,

00:52:30.306 --> 00:52:34.750
or a slight majority
collude, or none collude.

00:52:34.751 --> 00:52:37.615
But you can also imagine
information theoretic scheme,

00:52:37.616 --> 00:52:40.675
or all but one collude
also but you kind of get

00:52:40.676 --> 00:52:44.570
into heavier
communication costs.

00:52:44.571 --> 00:52:47.225
And then, computational
PIR is like

00:52:47.226 --> 00:52:50.405
a different scheme that's like
a trustless scheme, right?

00:52:50.406 --> 00:52:55.765
And so, and their communication

00:52:55.766 --> 00:52:59.875
and computation costs
are much higher.

00:52:59.876 --> 00:53:06.440
&gt;&gt; So, FSS is your core
technique in some sense critical.

00:53:06.441 --> 00:53:09.745
&gt;&gt; That's a core like
crypto building block, yeah.

00:53:09.746 --> 00:53:14.030
&gt;&gt; Different PIR has
some other crypto primitives.

00:53:14.031 --> 00:53:17.215
So, what is the key when
you compare them that

00:53:17.216 --> 00:53:20.940
enables you to be
better than PIR schemes?

00:53:20.941 --> 00:53:25.415
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think the
biggest difference between,

00:53:25.416 --> 00:53:28.475
I think funny to sharing is
like just very different.

00:53:28.476 --> 00:53:32.875
Because one it's like
an expressive PIR,

00:53:32.876 --> 00:53:34.695
or kind of like
if you think of it

00:53:34.696 --> 00:53:37.215
because now you don't
have to have an index,

00:53:37.216 --> 00:53:39.015
you kind of can match records,

00:53:39.016 --> 00:53:40.235
you can do less
than greater than,

00:53:40.236 --> 00:53:43.325
which is actually not
possible in PIR directly.

00:53:43.326 --> 00:53:45.925
Like of course, you can play
some tricks to make it work.

00:53:45.926 --> 00:53:48.665
And I think their information

00:53:48.666 --> 00:53:50.375
directly are, they do xSource.

00:53:50.376 --> 00:53:51.805
And so, they kind of require

00:53:51.806 --> 00:53:53.625
multiple servers
to different model

00:53:53.626 --> 00:53:55.545
but in computational PIR,

00:53:55.546 --> 00:53:57.655
you have to use
public key cryptography,

00:53:57.656 --> 00:53:58.875
and we use one wave function.

00:53:58.876 --> 00:54:01.170
So, I think we kind
of stand in the middle

00:54:01.171 --> 00:54:05.125
between in terms of kind of

00:54:05.126 --> 00:54:06.640
assumptions wise between

00:54:06.641 --> 00:54:08.230
cryptographic
assumptions between

00:54:08.231 --> 00:54:11.900
information theoretic
and computational PIR.

00:54:11.901 --> 00:54:14.285
So, I think it's
just a different model.

00:54:14.286 --> 00:54:16.155
I think it's hard to directly

00:54:16.156 --> 00:54:18.145
compare because they all
provide different features.

00:54:18.146 --> 00:54:20.565
But I think the expressiveness
is the very key features.

00:54:20.566 --> 00:54:22.855
And the expressiveness with

00:54:22.856 --> 00:54:25.635
the kind of like what
we call any trust,

00:54:25.636 --> 00:54:28.995
or like one non-colluding
server assumption is like,

00:54:28.996 --> 00:54:30.815
I think very strong.

00:54:30.816 --> 00:54:34.545
I mean, an interesting and
kind of like model to look at.

00:54:34.546 --> 00:54:37.295
&gt;&gt; Quick question.
So, the advantage

00:54:37.296 --> 00:54:38.805
of using three servers over

00:54:38.806 --> 00:54:40.735
two servers is that it's harder

00:54:40.736 --> 00:54:41.855
for three servers to collude

00:54:41.856 --> 00:54:42.895
because they need
to all collude.

00:54:42.896 --> 00:54:44.375
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, in that case,

00:54:44.376 --> 00:54:46.180
like two servers
can be malicious.

00:54:46.181 --> 00:54:48.217
&gt;&gt;Two servers, is
there any other?

00:54:48.218 --> 00:54:54.230
other than [inaudible] to use
three servers rather than two.

00:54:54.231 --> 00:54:55.555
I think, the clue thing
is the main thing.

00:54:55.556 --> 00:54:57.565
It's a little bit faster for

00:54:57.566 --> 00:54:59.680
larger databases just because

00:54:59.681 --> 00:55:01.475
the scheme is a
little bit different,

00:55:01.476 --> 00:55:03.280
but the disadvantage
with why it's a

00:55:03.281 --> 00:55:05.140
little bit faster is like now,

00:55:05.141 --> 00:55:07.125
you have more
communication costs

00:55:07.126 --> 00:55:12.215
because just current FSS
with three or more parties,

00:55:12.216 --> 00:55:15.500
the factor depends on
the number of parties,

00:55:15.501 --> 00:55:17.120
and where in two-server doesn't.

00:55:17.121 --> 00:55:19.290
So it's just kind of
have the scheme works.

00:55:19.291 --> 00:55:21.195
I think it's a very new scheme.

00:55:21.196 --> 00:55:22.920
I think there's a lot
of work that needs

00:55:22.921 --> 00:55:24.495
to be done both on
that side and kind of like

00:55:24.496 --> 00:55:31.340
thinking more about what's
going on. So continue.

00:55:31.341 --> 00:55:32.680
Garbled circuits, they are

00:55:32.681 --> 00:55:34.790
secure computation tool through

00:55:34.791 --> 00:55:36.560
parties to privately evaluate

00:55:36.561 --> 00:55:38.550
a function by
using this tool has

00:55:38.551 --> 00:55:41.270
very high computation
and communication costs

00:55:41.271 --> 00:55:43.680
in our specific setting.

00:55:43.681 --> 00:55:46.120
And so to a kind of conclude

00:55:46.121 --> 00:55:48.035
this Splinter part of the talk,

00:55:48.036 --> 00:55:49.470
so current web services leak

00:55:49.471 --> 00:55:51.380
sensitive information
to user queries.

00:55:51.381 --> 00:55:52.880
I presented to
you Splinter which

00:55:52.881 --> 00:55:54.780
is the first system that uses

00:55:54.781 --> 00:55:56.460
an extends FSS with

00:55:56.461 --> 00:55:59.310
new protocols to efficiently
protect these queries.

00:55:59.311 --> 00:56:02.155
Splinter-enabled versions
of realistic applications

00:56:02.156 --> 00:56:03.775
are fast and secure.

00:56:03.776 --> 00:56:06.430
And so I've now to talk
to you about Splinter.

00:56:06.431 --> 00:56:10.110
I'll talk to you a little
bit about future work.

00:56:10.880 --> 00:56:15.145
It's fashionable.

00:56:15.146 --> 00:56:18.695
So have you evaluated under
mutational cost [inaudible].

00:56:18.696 --> 00:56:20.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So we do
have in our paper.

00:56:20.561 --> 00:56:22.700
So we can look at Amazon costs.

00:56:22.701 --> 00:56:24.435
So I know one query.

00:56:24.436 --> 00:56:25.700
If It's not a Maps query,

00:56:25.701 --> 00:56:29.690
it's a regular query,
costs about ¢0.01.

00:56:29.691 --> 00:56:31.405
It's like less than a cent,

00:56:31.406 --> 00:56:33.570
ensures a computational cost in

00:56:33.571 --> 00:56:35.960
terms like if you do
a kind of timing match.

00:56:35.961 --> 00:56:38.510
I mean we did this a lot of

00:56:38.511 --> 00:56:41.100
research before
the times of serverless.

00:56:41.101 --> 00:56:42.560
And so I think if you run on

