00:56:48.306 --> 00:56:50.835
It was less than ¢0.01.

00:56:50.836 --> 00:56:54.950
But the result
privacy protection,

00:56:54.951 --> 00:56:56.690
how much will it cost?

00:56:56.691 --> 00:56:59.200
It will be very fast.

00:56:59.201 --> 00:57:01.480
I think it's hard to really
make that comparison.

00:57:01.481 --> 00:57:02.245
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:57:02.246 --> 00:57:05.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah, about probably
three orders of magnitude.

00:57:05.081 --> 00:57:08.360
The reason is because we

00:57:08.361 --> 00:57:10.260
can't do a lot of
the traditional tricks

00:57:10.261 --> 00:57:11.320
that a database can.

00:57:11.321 --> 00:57:14.350
So like a database
heavily engineered,

00:57:14.351 --> 00:57:16.550
like they have
scope of everything.

00:57:16.551 --> 00:57:19.470
They can do query planning
that we cannot,

00:57:19.471 --> 00:57:21.300
they can have optimized rows,

00:57:21.301 --> 00:57:22.350
and they have hundreds of

00:57:22.351 --> 00:57:24.020
engineers who are going to type,

00:57:24.021 --> 00:57:27.550
optimize the crap out of
a database, and so on.

00:57:27.551 --> 00:57:30.705
And they're very cognizant
performance, etc., etc.

00:57:30.706 --> 00:57:33.520
Josh, this scheme is
very caching-unfriendly.

00:57:33.521 --> 00:57:35.450
Yeah, it's very
caching unfriendly.

00:57:35.451 --> 00:57:38.445
We do some caching,
but very unfriendly,

00:57:38.446 --> 00:57:40.595
like you have to
pay storage costs.

00:57:40.596 --> 00:57:45.720
I think it's because it's
like you have security,

00:57:45.721 --> 00:57:49.250
you don't have a view of
certain amounts of information.

00:57:49.251 --> 00:57:52.580
Like in a sequel databases,

00:57:52.581 --> 00:57:57.410
if you know everything,
you can plan for it.

00:57:57.411 --> 00:58:02.895
Yeah. So here's some kind
of follow on work.

00:58:02.896 --> 00:58:04.880
So Splinter is
reduced overhead for

00:58:04.881 --> 00:58:07.525
private carries
by leveraging FSS.

00:58:07.526 --> 00:58:09.100
But it's really
only the beginning.

00:58:09.101 --> 00:58:10.750
I think some of you guys brought

00:58:10.751 --> 00:58:12.610
up some kind of
follow on work or

00:58:12.611 --> 00:58:16.480
some interesting work
that needs to be done.

00:58:16.481 --> 00:58:20.180
The amount of data in these
web services is only growing,

00:58:20.181 --> 00:58:24.515
and users expect
more expressive queries.

00:58:24.516 --> 00:58:27.780
And so it's not clear
how we can scale

00:58:27.781 --> 00:58:31.035
the billions or trillions
of records with this scheme,

00:58:31.036 --> 00:58:33.130
how we can have
more expressive queries

00:58:33.131 --> 00:58:34.430
like joins and knots,

00:58:34.431 --> 00:58:36.060
how we improve efficiency for

00:58:36.061 --> 00:58:37.700
really four or more servers,

00:58:37.701 --> 00:58:39.595
like I said, for two or three,
it's pretty efficient,

00:58:39.596 --> 00:58:42.040
and from four, it doesn't
scale as well because

00:58:42.041 --> 00:58:46.020
FSS technique does not scale
as well past four servers.

00:58:46.021 --> 00:58:47.750
And so some future directions,

00:58:47.751 --> 00:58:49.460
we've been thinking
about and trying to make

00:58:49.461 --> 00:58:52.230
more progress on is like how
can we do some forward PAR,

00:58:52.231 --> 00:58:54.530
FSS with pre-processing
so we don't have to

00:58:54.531 --> 00:58:57.060
look through the whole database
every single time.

00:58:57.061 --> 00:58:58.190
So I think that helps on

00:58:58.191 --> 00:59:00.265
the billions or trillions
of records part,

00:59:00.266 --> 00:59:02.500
like how can we design
a more expressive FSS

00:59:02.501 --> 00:59:04.300
to solve the expressive
queries part.

00:59:04.301 --> 00:59:08.000
Maybe this just requires
new cryptographic tools for

00:59:08.001 --> 00:59:09.685
systems or new cryptographic

00:59:09.686 --> 00:59:13.605
Pierre FSS scheme, like schemes.

00:59:13.606 --> 00:59:17.215
And so on a related topic PIR

00:59:17.216 --> 00:59:20.245
and FSS and multiparty
computation,

00:59:20.246 --> 00:59:22.650
MPC have pretty
strong connections.

00:59:22.651 --> 00:59:25.080
And in FSS, we wanted
to hide the function,

00:59:25.081 --> 00:59:26.495
but the input was public.

00:59:26.496 --> 00:59:28.570
However, MPCs are
reverse problem.

00:59:28.571 --> 00:59:29.950
We want to hide the input,

00:59:29.951 --> 00:59:31.645
but the function is public.

00:59:31.646 --> 00:59:33.650
And so, more concretely,

00:59:33.651 --> 00:59:34.870
we have four values,

00:59:34.871 --> 00:59:36.890
X1 through X4, and we

00:59:36.891 --> 00:59:39.520
want to compute
a known function on it.

00:59:39.521 --> 00:59:41.560
However, the servers want

00:59:41.561 --> 00:59:42.780
to know the result
of the function,

00:59:42.781 --> 00:59:44.950
but they don't want to
reveal their respective input

00:59:44.951 --> 00:59:47.510
to other servers.

00:59:47.511 --> 00:59:51.870
And so why is this useful?

00:59:51.871 --> 00:59:53.390
Organizations are storing

00:59:53.391 --> 00:59:55.175
more and more data in the cloud,

00:59:55.176 --> 00:59:57.400
and data is becoming
more digitize.

00:59:57.401 --> 00:59:59.705
However, many times
organizations

00:59:59.706 --> 01:00:01.230
might want to keep their data

01:00:01.231 --> 01:00:03.440
proprietary but might
be willing to compute

01:00:03.441 --> 01:00:05.810
a shared function or
selectively store data.

01:00:05.811 --> 01:00:07.545
And so, for example,

01:00:07.546 --> 01:00:09.180
patients might visit

01:00:09.181 --> 01:00:11.585
different hospitals
for various conditions.

01:00:11.586 --> 01:00:13.940
As a result, not one hospital

01:00:13.941 --> 01:00:16.175
has a complete view of
the patient's health.

01:00:16.176 --> 01:00:19.155
So imagine this for
millions of patients.

01:00:19.156 --> 01:00:22.530
Similarly, many patients might
have a similar condition,

01:00:22.531 --> 01:00:24.770
but a single hospital
will only have

01:00:24.771 --> 01:00:27.200
a sparse data set to
do research on this.

01:00:27.201 --> 01:00:29.330
But across many hospitals,

01:00:29.331 --> 01:00:30.810
there could be a richer dataset.

01:00:30.811 --> 01:00:32.490
And so in the current world,

01:00:32.491 --> 01:00:34.300
medical researchers
could be missing

01:00:34.301 --> 01:00:37.995
key insights because
the data is so fragmented.

01:00:37.996 --> 01:00:40.845
But with MPC you have a way

01:00:40.846 --> 01:00:43.885
to share and analyze
this data in a secure way.

01:00:43.886 --> 01:00:46.980
And so the reason MPC is
not used frequently in

01:00:46.981 --> 01:00:48.350
practice is because of

01:00:48.351 --> 01:00:51.635
a poor performance both in
computation and bandwidth.

01:00:51.636 --> 01:00:54.160
So an interesting future
direction is to make

01:00:54.161 --> 01:00:56.900
garbled circuit systems
more efficient.

01:00:56.901 --> 01:00:59.540
Some recent work out of
Berkeley has shown that

01:00:59.541 --> 01:01:01.095
corporate circuits can be

01:01:01.096 --> 01:01:02.960
somewhat practically in systems,

01:01:02.961 --> 01:01:04.825
but they have
limited functionality.

01:01:04.826 --> 01:01:07.580
The goal is to kind of
expand the functionality

01:01:07.581 --> 01:01:08.800
of the garbled circuit systems

01:01:08.801 --> 01:01:10.340
and make them more practical.

01:01:10.341 --> 01:01:12.750
Similarly, there's
related FSS technique

01:01:12.751 --> 01:01:14.460
called homomorphic
circuit sharing,

01:01:14.461 --> 01:01:16.030
which kind of reverses this.

01:01:16.031 --> 01:01:17.250
And so for them,

01:01:17.251 --> 01:01:18.575
the function is public,

01:01:18.576 --> 01:01:20.260
but the input is private also,

01:01:20.261 --> 01:01:23.125
and they do that for
branching programs.

01:01:23.126 --> 01:01:26.020
And so you can kind of

01:01:26.021 --> 01:01:27.330
imagine it being more expressive

01:01:27.331 --> 01:01:29.025
with more functionality also.

01:01:29.026 --> 01:01:31.940
And another extension
for MPC is to find

01:01:31.941 --> 01:01:34.780
ways to maybe do MPC on
graphs more efficiently,

01:01:34.781 --> 01:01:37.610
which is a common data
structure commonly

01:01:37.611 --> 01:01:39.900
insecurity as people try

01:01:39.901 --> 01:01:42.140
to contextualize and
respond to threats.

01:01:42.141 --> 01:01:44.470
And so I'm excited to

01:01:44.471 --> 01:01:46.760
continue collaborating
with both cryptographers

01:01:46.761 --> 01:01:50.090
and systems researchers to
solve these problems and find

01:01:50.091 --> 01:01:51.880
new systems applications for

01:01:51.881 --> 01:01:54.085
a lot of these
cryptographic tools.

